  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Lecture core · Center, then form covariance",
      "body": "For rows $x_i$, compute $\\mu=\\frac1N\\sum_i x_i$ and $z_i=x_i-\\mu$. The sample covariance used here is $C=\\frac1N\\sum_i z_i z_i^\\top$. The factor convention may vary, but the eigenvectors are unchanged by a positive scalar factor.",
      "sourceType": "lecture",
      "math": {
        "latex": "Z=X-\\mathbf{1}\\mu^\\top,\\qquad C=\\frac1N Z^\\top Z",
        "explanation": "The centering step removes the mean offset. The covariance is symmetric positive semidefinite, so its eigenvalues are non-negative and its eigenvectors can be chosen orthonormal.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\mu",
            "meaning": "feature mean"
          },
          {
            "latex": "Z",
            "meaning": "centered data matrix"
          },
          {
            "latex": "C",
            "meaning": "sample covariance"
          }
        ],
        "name": "PCA centering and covariance",
        "purpose": "Use it to construct the covariance matrix whose eigenvectors define the principal directions.",
        "sourceType": "lecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · Variance and Rayleigh quotient",
      "body": "The variance after projecting onto a unit vector $v$ is $v^\\top Cv$. Maximizing this quadratic form gives the leading eigenvector. Repeating with orthogonality constraints gives subsequent principal directions.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "v_1=\\arg\\max_{\\lVert v\\rVert_2=1}v^\\top Cv",
        "explanation": "The unit-norm constraint removes the trivial scaling solution. The maximum equals the largest eigenvalue of C.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "v_1",
            "meaning": "first principal direction"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\lambda_1",
            "meaning": "largest covariance eigenvalue"
          }
        ],
        "name": "PCA Rayleigh quotient",
        "purpose": "Use it to turn maximum projected variance into a constrained eigenvalue problem.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · Reconstruction error",
      "body": "Project onto the span of the first $k$ eigenvectors and reconstruct. The total squared error discarded by PCA is the sum of the remaining eigenvalues. This gives a numerical way to choose $k$ and explains why the largest eigenvalue is the best one-dimensional direction.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\operatorname{MSE}_{recon}(k)\\propto\\sum_{j>k}\\lambda_j",
        "explanation": "Keeping more principal directions removes more reconstruction error. The exact normalization depends on whether the loss is summed or averaged.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "k",
            "meaning": "number of kept components"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\lambda_j",
            "meaning": "ordered covariance eigenvalues"
          }
        ],
        "name": "PCA reconstruction-error spectrum",
        "purpose": "Use it to connect discarded eigenvalues with the error introduced by retaining only a chosen number of components.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reference boundary · Whitening and exam bridge",
      "body": "Whitening maps principal coordinates by $\\Lambda^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$, producing unit covariance for retained non-zero directions. The past exam also asks for an SVM-like constrained formulation of the maximum-variance direction; treat that as an assessment bridge and derive it from the Rayleigh quotient rather than memorizing a disconnected formula.",
      "sourceType": "ref",
      "math": {
        "latex": "X_{white}=ZV\\Lambda^{-1/2}",
        "explanation": "V contains the orthonormal eigenvectors and Lambda the diagonal eigenvalue matrix. Small eigenvalues may amplify noise, so practical whitening often clips or regularizes them.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "V",
            "meaning": "eigenvector matrix"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\Lambda",
            "meaning": "diagonal eigenvalue matrix"
          }
        ],
        "name": "PCA whitening transform",
        "purpose": "Use it to rescale retained principal coordinates to unit variance, checking small-eigenvalue stability.",
        "sourceType": "ref"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Assessment habit · Sign and scaling conventions",
      "body": "An eigenvector and its negative represent the same PCA axis. Covariance may be divided by N or N-1 depending on convention; compare the direction and relative eigenvalues first, then follow the convention stated in the question.",
      "sourceType": "ref"
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "title": "2 by 2 covariance",
      "steps": [
        "Let $C=[[2,1],[1,2]]$. Its eigenvectors are $(1,1)/\\sqrt2$ and $(1,-1)/\\sqrt2$.",
        "The corresponding eigenvalues are 3 and 1, so the first component is the diagonal direction (1,1).",
        "A one-dimensional reconstruction discards the perpendicular eigenvalue 1; the kept variance is 3 and the discarded variance is 1 under the same normalization."
      ],
      "answer": "For a symmetric covariance matrix, use orthogonal eigenvectors and sort by decreasing eigenvalue.",
      "sourceType": "textbook"
    }
  ],
  "criticalQuestions": [
    {
      "prompt": "Why center before PCA?",
      "focus": "geometry",
      "angle": "separate location from variation",
      "modelAnswer": "PCA should describe variation around the empirical mean. Without centering, a large offset from the origin can dominate the covariance-like second moment and change the selected direction."
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Why does the top eigenvalue matter for one-dimensional PCA?",
      "focus": "optimization",
      "angle": "connect Rayleigh quotient and error",
      "modelAnswer": "The leading eigenvalue is the maximum projected variance, and the sum of the remaining eigenvalues is the minimum discarded squared reconstruction variance for a one-dimensional subspace."
    }
  ],
